<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s09e03" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 3: Interpreting Graphs</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 3 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a graph representing a real-world relationship, answer questions and make conclusions using the graph.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Remember that a graph is a visual representation of the relationship between two quantities. 
    If you have a graph, you can answer lots of different questions about the two quantities.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      The following graph shows the relationship between the year (on the horizontal axis) and the population of a certain town (on the vertical axis).
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Graph showing population in a town over time</caption>
      <image source="93a.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>The lower left of the graph shows a population of about 10 thousand in 1801 
        in the lower left corner and the population grows in roughly a strait line to a population of almost 160 thousand in 2011 in the upper right
        of the graph.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Part 1-A:	What was the town’s population during the year 1891?
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution 1-A:	Start with the given information: Look for <m>y=1</m> on the vertical axis. 
        Then, navigate to the point on the graph that has a vertical coordinate of 1. Then, look to the horizontal axis to see the <m>x</m>-value.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Graph showing population over time, with a highlight at time 1891</caption>
      <image source="93b.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>The same as the previous graph with the point (1891, 60 thousand) circled.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
      
    <p>
      Part 1-B:	When did the population reach 100 thousand people?
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1-B:	Start with the given information: Look for 100 (thousand) on the vertical axis. 
      Find the point on the graph with that vertical coordinate. Then look down to the horizontal axis for the year.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Graph showing population over time, with a highlight at population 100 thousand</caption>
        <image source="93c.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>The same as the previous graph with the point (1940, 100 thousand) circled.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
     The year on the vertical axis is between 1921 and 1951, slightly closer to 1951. 
      So, a reasonable answer is that the population reached 100 thousand in approximately the year 1940. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Below is a graph of some equation relating two quantities <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A graph of a linear equation</caption>
        <image source="93d.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the line y = -two thirds x plus two graphed</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Part 1-A: Find the value of <m>x</m> that is paired with <m>y=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1-A:	Start with the given information: Look for <m>y=1</m> on the vertical axis. 
      Then, navigate to the point on the graph that has a vertical coordinate of 1. Then, look to the horizontal axis to see the <m>x</m>-value.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A graph of a linear equation with y = 1 highlighted</caption>
        <image source="93e.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the line y = -two thirds x plus two graphed with the point (1.5, 1) highlighted</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The value on the horizontal axis is between 1 and 2. So a reasonable answer would be that <m>x=1.5</m> is the <m>x</m>-value that is paired with <m>y=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Part 1-B: Find the value of <m>y</m> that is paired with <m>x=</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1-B:	Start with the given information: Find <m>x=0</m> on the horizontal axis. 
      Then, navigate to the point on the graph that has a horizontal coordinate of 0. Then, look to the vertical axis to find the <m>y</m>-value.
    </p>
<figure>
      <caption>A graph of a linear equation with x=0 highlighted</caption>
        <image source="93f.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the line y = -two thirds x plus two graphed with the point (0, 2) highlighted</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The point has a vertical coordinate of 2. So <m>x=0</m> is paired with a <m>y</m>-value of 2.
    </p>
    <p>
      Part 1-C:	Find the value of <m>x</m> that is paired with <m>y=0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Solution 1-C:	Start with the given information: Find <m>y=0</m> on the vertical axis. 
        Then, navigate to the point on the graph that has a vertical coordinate of 0. Then, look to the horizontal axis to find the <m>x</m>-value.
      </p>
      <figure>
      <caption>A graph of a linear equation with y=0 highlighted</caption>
        <image source="93g.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the line y = -two thirds x plus two graphed with the point (3, 0) highlighted</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The point has a horizontal coordinate of 3. So <m>x=3</m> is paired with <m>y=0</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Notice Something Neat!</title>
    <p>
      If the x-coordinate is <m>x=0</m>, the point lies on the y-axis.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the y-coordinate is <m>y=0</m>, the point lies on the x-axis.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Below is a graph of some equation relating two quantities <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A graph of the parabola <m>y=x^2</m></caption>
        <image source="93h.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the parabola x squared</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Part 1-A:	Find the value of <m>y</m> paired with <m>x=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1-A:	Start with the given information: look for <m>x=1</m> on the horizontal axis. 
      Navigate to the point on the graph with that horizontal coordinate. Then look over to the y-axis for the vertical coordinate. 
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A graph of the parabola with x = 1 highlighted<m>y=x^2</m></caption>
        <image source="93i.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the parabola x squared with the point (1,1) circled</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The value <m>x=1</m> is paired with the value <m>y=1</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Find the value(s) of <m>x</m> paired with <m>y=4</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1-B:	Start with the given information and look for <m>y=4</m> on the vertical axis. 
      There are two points on the graph with that vertical coordinate. We look down to the x-axis for their horizontal coordinates.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A graph of the parabola with y = 4 highlighted<m>y=x^2</m></caption>
        <image source="93j.png" width = "60%">
        <shortdescription>the parabola x squared with the points (2,4) and (-2,4) circled</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The x-values <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=2</m> are paired with <m>y=4</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s09e03">
    <title>Season 9, Episode 3 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <p>
        For the following graph find the the y-value when <m>x=4</m> AND find the x-value when <m>y=-1</m>
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of the line y = x over 2 minus 1 </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-93ex1" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=x/2-1 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <p>
        For the following graph find the the y-value when <m>x=3</m> AND find the x-value(s) when <m>y=3</m>
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of the line y = -2x+3</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-93ex2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=-2*x+3</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <p>
        For the following graph find the the y-value when <m>x=-1</m> AND find the x-value(s) when <m>y=2</m>
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = x squared minus two </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-93ex3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=x^2-2 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <p>
        For the following graph find the the y-value when <m>x=0</m> AND find the x-value when <m>y=1</m>
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
  <shortdescription>A plot of y = x cubed </shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-93ex4" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
                  <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x)=x^3/8 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
          <graph function="f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <image source="93k.PNG" width = "80%">
        <shortdescription>a line graph depicting juan's bike ride with points at (0,0), (30,6),(90,12),(135,0) where x represents time in minutes and y 
        represents distance in miles</shortdescription>
      </image>
    <p>
      How far from home is Juan after 75 minutes? At what times was Juan 3 miles from home?
    </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <image source="93l.PNG" width = "80%">
        <shortdescription>a line graph depicting the number of teachers at jones high with points at
         (1990,20), (1995,30),(2000,50),(2005,50),(2010,40),(2015,60),(2020,80) where x represents the year and y 
        represents number of teachers</shortdescription>
      </image>
    <p>
      How many teachers were there in 1995? How many teachers were there in 2015? In what years were there 40 teachers at Jones High?
    </p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section>  